logging: `ip_mask` logs `<nil>` for IPv6 clients when only an IPv4 mask is set

### Issue Details

With the `ip_mask` log filter, an address whose family has no CIDR configured is logged as `<nil>`. A config that only sets an IPv4 size, which looks complete, loses every IPv6 client:

```
{
	auto_https off
}
http://:8080 {
	log {
		output file access.log
		format filter {
			wrap json
			fields {
				request>remote_ip ip_mask 16
				request>client_ip ip_mask {
					ipv4 24
				}
			}
		}
	}
	respond "ok"
}
```

```
$ curl -g 'http://[::1]:8080/'
$ jq -c '.request | [.remote_ip, .client_ip]' access.log
["<nil>","<nil>"]
```

An out-of-range size is accepted without an error and has the same effect: `ip_mask 33` passes `caddy validate` and then logs `<nil>` for every IPv4 address.

Version: v2.11.7 (release binary) and master at 05848752.

Cause: in `modules/logging/filters.go`, `IPMaskFilter.Provision` maps an unset (0) size to a nil mask, and `net.CIDRMask` also returns nil for a size outside 0-32 / 0-128. `mask()` then calls `ip.Mask(nil)`, which returns nil, and its `String()` is `<nil>`.

The out-of-range case looks like a plain config error, so I have a small patch with a test that rejects it at provision time.

For the unset family I'm not sure what you want: leave the address unchanged (an unconfigured family is not masked), or mask all of it (`::` / `0.0.0.0`) so nothing that is hidden today gets logged. Happy to add either to the patch.

### Assistance Disclosure

AI used

### If AI was used, describe the extent to which it was used.

AI used (Claude Code).

