issue:
Connection.prototype.bulkWrite() with ordered: false resolves null when a write fails

### Prerequisites

- [x] I have written a descriptive issue title
- [x] I have searched existing issues to ensure the bug has not already been reported

### Mongoose version

9.11.2

### Node.js version

22.23.3

### MongoDB server version

8.2.6

### Typescript version (if applicable)

_No response_

### Description

With `ordered: false`, `Connection.prototype.bulkWrite()` resolves with `null` when the server rejects one of the writes (a duplicate key here, but also write concern errors and network errors). The caller gets no error and no result, so the failed write is easy to miss. The same ops with `ordered: true` reject with `MongoClientBulkWriteError`, and `Model.bulkWrite()` with `ordered: false` rejects with `MongoBulkWriteError`.

In the unordered branch, `this.client.bulkWrite(validOps, options)` is wrapped in `.catch(err => ([null, err]))`. The `if (error)` block after it only attaches `validationErrors` to the error and never throws it, so the method returns `res`, which is `null`:

https://github.com/Automattic/mongoose/blob/0f746df5071f7f290723150bd8633d7be9342cb1/lib/connection.js#L572-L602

If one of the ops also fails validation (and `throwOnValidationError` is not set), the call rejects with `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'mongoose')` instead, because `decorateBulkWriteResult()` is called with the `null` result.

### Steps to Reproduce

```js
'use strict';

const mongoose = require('mongoose');

run().catch(err => {
  console.error(err);
  process.exit(1);
});

async function run() {
  // Connection.prototype.bulkWrite() needs MongoDB server 8.0 or later
  await mongoose.connect('mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/mongoose_test');

  const Test = mongoose.model('Test', new mongoose.Schema({ _id: Number, name: String }));
  await Test.deleteMany({});
  await Test.create({ _id: 1, name: 'existing' });

  const ops = [
    { model: Test, name: 'insertOne', document: { _id: 2, name: 'new' } },
    { model: Test, name: 'insertOne', document: { _id: 1, name: 'duplicate' } }
  ];

  const res = await mongoose.connection.bulkWrite(ops, { ordered: false });
  console.log('resolved with', res); // null, no error
  console.log('documents', await Test.countDocuments()); // 2: the duplicate was not written

  await mongoose.disconnect();
}
```

Output:

```
resolved with null
documents 2
```

### Expected Behavior

`bulkWrite()` rejects with the driver's `MongoClientBulkWriteError`, like the ordered case and `Model.bulkWrite()` do, after the other ops have been applied. When there are also validation errors, they are attached to that error as `error.mongoose.validationErrors`, which the `if (error)` block already does.

The fix I have in mind is to throw `error` at the end of that block. I can open a PR with a failing test.

