issue:
bulkWrite() doesn't cast arrayFilters, so a string _id in an array filter matches nothing

### Prerequisites

- [x] I have written a descriptive issue title
- [x] I have searched existing issues to ensure the bug has not already been reported

### Mongoose version

9.11.1

### Node.js version

24.16.0

### MongoDB server version

8.2.6

### Typescript version (if applicable)

_No response_

### Description

`Model.bulkWrite()` sends `arrayFilters` to MongoDB without casting them, while `Model.updateOne()` and `updateMany()` cast them against the schema. So with a string `_id`, a number passed as a string or a date string in an array filter, `updateOne()` updates the element but the same update in `bulkWrite()` modifies nothing. There's no error, so it's easy to miss.

`castUpdateOne()` and `castUpdateMany()` in `lib/helpers/model/castBulkWrite.js` pass `arrayFilters` to `castUpdate()` (that was added in #15036 for embedded discriminator paths), but nothing casts the filter values themselves. On the query side, `castArrayFilters()` does that. `Connection.prototype.bulkWrite()` uses the same functions, so it has the same problem.

Also happens on 8.24.5.

### Steps to Reproduce

```js
'use strict';

const mongoose = require('mongoose');

run().catch(err => {
  console.error(err);
  process.exit(1);
});

async function run() {
  await mongoose.connect('mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/mongoose_test');

  const Order = mongoose.model('Order', new mongoose.Schema({
    items: [{ name: String, qty: Number, shippedAt: Date }]
  }));
  await Order.deleteMany({});

  const order = await Order.create({
    items: [{ name: 'apple', qty: 1, shippedAt: new Date('2026-01-10') }]
  });
  const itemId = order.items[0]._id.toString(); // e.g. from req.params

  const cases = [
    ['_id as string', { 'item._id': itemId }],
    ['number as string', { 'item.qty': '1' }],
    ['date as string', { 'item.shippedAt': { $gte: '2026-01-01' } }]
  ];

  for (const [label, arrayFilter] of cases) {
    const update = { $set: { 'items.$[item].name': 'pear' } };

    await Order.updateOne({ _id: order._id }, { $set: { 'items.0.name': 'apple' } });
    const res1 = await Order.updateOne({ _id: order._id }, update, { arrayFilters: [arrayFilter] });

    await Order.updateOne({ _id: order._id }, { $set: { 'items.0.name': 'apple' } });
    const res2 = await Order.bulkWrite([
      { updateOne: { filter: { _id: order._id }, update, arrayFilters: [arrayFilter] } }
    ]);

    console.log(`${label}: updateOne() modified ${res1.modifiedCount}, bulkWrite() modified ${res2.modifiedCount}`);
  }

  console.log('mongoose', mongoose.version);
  await mongoose.disconnect();
}
```

Output:

```
_id as string: updateOne() modified 1, bulkWrite() modified 0
number as string: updateOne() modified 1, bulkWrite() modified 0
date as string: updateOne() modified 1, bulkWrite() modified 0
mongoose 9.11.1
```

### Expected Behavior

`bulkWrite()` casts `arrayFilters` the same way `updateOne()` and `updateMany()` do, so all three cases modify 1 document.

